\section{Method}

We fine-tune OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024openvla} with the OFT recipe~\cite{kim2025oft}, which concatenates DINOv2~\cite{oquab2024dinov2} (1024-d) and SigLIP~\cite{zhai2023siglip} (1152-d) patch features into a 2176-dimensional representation per spatial token. We apply LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora} (rank 32, $\alpha{=}16$) and augment the action prediction loss with an auxiliary spatial prediction head:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_\text{total} = \mathcal{L}_\text{action} + \lambda(t) \cdot \mathcal{L}_\text{aux}(h(\phi_v(o_t)), s_t^*),
\quad \lambda(t) = 0.01 \cdot \exp(-t / 50000)
\end{equation}
where $h$ denotes the auxiliary head, $\phi_v$ the frozen-then-LoRA-adapted vision encoders, and $s_t^*$ the ground-truth spatial label at step $t$. The coefficient $\lambda(t)$ decays exponentially so that spatial gradients shape early representations without dominating late-stage action refinement. The auxiliary head is discarded at inference, incurring zero additional cost.

\textbf{BEV occupancy head.} The head (1.2M parameters) maps the 256 patch tokens from the current observation frame to a $64{\times}64$ binary occupancy grid. A linear projection ($2176{\to}256$) is reshaped to $16{\times}16$, followed by two transposed convolutions ($256{\to}128{\to}64$, kernel 4, stride 2) each with BatchNorm2d and GELU, and a final $1{\times}1$ convolution with sigmoid activation. Ground-truth grids are rendered from MuJoCo simulator state replay by projecting object bounding boxes onto the table-plane BEV. Training uses focal loss~\cite{lin2017focal} ($\alpha{=}0.25$, $\gamma{=}2.0$) to handle class imbalance in the sparse occupancy signal. The initial auxiliary weight $\lambda_0{=}0.01$ is set to keep auxiliary gradients below 2\% of the action loss magnitude, avoiding interference with action learning.

\textbf{Monocular depth head.} This head shares the same convolutional architecture but replaces the sigmoid with ReLU and uses smooth-L1 loss. Ground-truth egocentric depth maps are rendered at $64{\times}64$ resolution from MuJoCo. The depth head provides an egocentric spatial reference frame, complementing the allocentric BEV occupancy grid.

\subsection{Ablation Design}
\label{sec:ablation}

We evaluate twelve conditions that systematically vary five factors: gradient flow from the auxiliary head into the vision encoders, label content, normalization type in the auxiliary head, spatial reference frame, and temporal extent of auxiliary supervision.

The key conditions are: \textbf{Vanilla} (action loss only, no auxiliary head); \textbf{Full OCC} (BEV occupancy with gradient flow through both encoders, BatchNorm2d); \textbf{Detach OCC} (same labels, encoder gradients blocked via \texttt{stop\_gradient}); and their LayerNorm variants (\textbf{Full OCC+LN}, \textbf{Detach+LN}). These four form a $2{\times}2$ factorial (gradient flow $\times$ normalization). Additional conditions include \textbf{Random OCC} (random binary labels, isolating gradient regularization from spatial content), \textbf{Depth} (egocentric depth maps), \textbf{Cutoff@\{15K--45K\}} (gradient flow for the first $N$ steps only; the post-cutoff phase retains the auxiliary head forward pass in Detach mode, see Appendix~\ref{app:training}), and \textbf{Reverse Cutoff} (gradient flow for the last 15K steps). Table~\ref{tab:ablation_design} summarizes the full design and the nine pairwise comparisons, each isolating a single factor.

